    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which home water filtration setup suits most households?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For city water, a whole-house catalytic carbon tank such as the SpringWell CF1, paired with an under-sink reverse osmosis unit for drinking water, covers most concerns. If budget is tight, a three-stage cartridge system like the iSpring WGB32B handles sediment and chlorine. Base the final choice on your utility report or lab test rather than marketing claims."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does a whole-house filter remove lead?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most whole-house carbon systems are not certified for lead, and lead often leaches from solder, faucets and fittings downstream of where a whole-house filter sits. The reliable fix is an NSF/ANSI 53 or 58 certified filter at the kitchen tap, plus replacing lead service lines where possible. Some whole-house systems offer lead-specific stages, so check certifications before relying on them."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a whole-house system lower my water pressure?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A correctly sized system with clean filters causes only a small pressure drop. Problems come from undersized systems, ports smaller than the main line, or clogged sediment cartridges. Match the rated flow to your household peak demand, install pressure gauges before and after, and change sediment filters when the pressure difference grows noticeably."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a softener and a filter?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They do different jobs. A softener removes the calcium and magnesium that cause scale, and a carbon filter removes chlorine, taste and many chemicals. If your water is hard and chlorinated, you need both. Install the carbon filter before the softener on chloramine or chlorine water, since chlorine degrades softener resin over time."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does professional installation cost?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "At the time of writing, plumbers typically charge roughly $150 to $400 to install an under-sink system and $300 to $1,000 for a standard whole-house filter. Well systems with backwashing tanks, iron filters and UV can run higher, especially if new drains or outlets are needed. Get quotes that include a bypass loop and bonding jumper."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is reverse osmosis water safe to drink long term?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. RO removes minerals as well as contaminants, but most dietary calcium and magnesium comes from food rather than water. Some people prefer the taste of water with minerals, and many RO systems offer a remineralization stage. Keep filters current, because an exhausted pre-filter can let chlorine reach and damage the membrane."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I filter well water with the same system as city water?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not. City water mainly needs chlorine or chloramine removal. Well water often needs sediment, iron, manganese and hardness treatment, and sometimes UV disinfection. Get a full lab panel first, then build the system in the right order: sediment, iron removal, softener, carbon, and UV last. A city-water carbon tank alone usually fails quickly on a well."
          }
        }
