          Home water filtration is the category where people most often spend $2,000 on the wrong solution. A whole-house carbon tank will not fix hard water, a softener will not remove lead, and reverse osmosis at the kitchen sink does nothing for the rotten-egg smell in the shower. Test the water first, decide whether the problem is at every tap or only the ones you drink from, and buy the smallest system that solves it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Home Water Filtration Systems, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Systems are ranked on published certifications and performance data, rated flow and capacity, installation demands, warranty terms, ongoing filter costs and recurring themes in owner feedback. Nothing here was bench tested for this list; specifications are attributed to manufacturer documentation and certification listings.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="test-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Test Your Water Before You Buy Anything
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every good filtration decision starts with numbers. On municipal water, pull your utility's annual <strong>Consumer Confidence Report</strong>, which lists every regulated contaminant detected at the plant and its level. That report tells you about chlorine or chloramine, disinfection byproducts, PFAS, nitrate and hardness at the source. What it cannot tell you is what happens inside your own pipes, so pair it with a lead test at your kitchen tap if the house was built before 1986.
          </p>

          <p>
            On a private well, nobody tests for you. The EPA and most state health departments recommend testing at least once a year for total coliform bacteria, nitrate, pH and total dissolved solids, and every few years for arsenic, uranium, iron, manganese and hardness depending on your region. Use a state-certified laboratory rather than a hardware-store strip kit. Lab panels typically cost $50 to $300 and give you numbers a system can be sized against.
          </p>

          <p>
            Home strip kits and TDS pens are useful for tracking change over time, but not for buying decisions. A TDS reading of 350 ppm tells you there is a lot dissolved in the water, not whether it is harmless calcium or something you should worry about. A hardness strip can confirm you need a softener, but it cannot tell you about lead or PFAS, which are measured in parts per billion or trillion.
          </p>

          <p>
            Write the results down and circle anything near or above a federal limit or a nuisance threshold. Iron above about 0.3 milligrams per liter stains fixtures orange. Hardness above roughly 7 grains per gallon builds scale. Hydrogen sulfide causes the sulfur smell. Each of those points to a specific kind of equipment, and the list you end up with is the shopping list for the rest of this guide.
          </p>

