        <div id="poe-vs-pou" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Whole-House or Point-of-Use?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A <strong>point-of-entry</strong> system, usually called whole-house, sits on the main line after the meter or pressure tank and treats every drop that enters the home: showers, washing machine, water heater and every faucet. A <strong>point-of-use</strong> system treats water at one tap, usually the kitchen sink, and only for drinking and cooking. Most homes are better served by one or the other, and a few need both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whole-house makes sense when the problem affects things other than drinking water. Chlorine or chloramine that dries out skin and hair, sediment that clogs aerators and appliance valves, iron that stains toilets and laundry, and sulfur smell in the shower are all point-of-entry problems. So is hard water scale, although that needs a softener rather than a filter. Large carbon tanks also handle chlorine taste at every tap, which keeps the household from needing pitchers everywhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            Point-of-use makes sense when the concern is something you drink. Lead, PFAS, arsenic, nitrate and fluoride matter when ingested, not when you shower. Treating them for the whole house is costly and wasteful, since perhaps one or two percent of a home's water is consumed. An under-sink reverse osmosis unit removes a wide range of dissolved contaminants for a fraction of the cost of a whole-house system that could do the same.
          </p>

          <p>
            The common combination is a whole-house sediment and carbon system for comfort and appliance protection, plus an under-sink RO or carbon block filter for drinking water. If you rent, cannot alter plumbing or only drink a few liters a day, a countertop RO unit or a certified pitcher covers the drinking side. Our <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">water filter pitcher rankings</a> cover the lowest-cost end of that spectrum.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE OF THUMB</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If it bothers you in the shower or stains fixtures, treat it at the main line. If it only matters when you drink it, treat it at the sink.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Most families that overspend bought whole-house treatment for a drinking-water contaminant. Most that are unhappy bought a sink filter for a problem that lives in every pipe.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="system-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. System Types, Explained Plainly
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Sediment filters</strong> are the first stage of almost every system. They catch sand, rust flakes and silt, rated in microns, typically 5 to 50 for whole-house use. Finer is not always better: a 5-micron cartridge on a sandy well can clog in weeks. Many installers run a coarse spin-down screen first, then a finer pleated cartridge. Sediment filtration protects every downstream stage and the appliances behind it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Carbon systems</strong> come in two forms. Cartridge housings, such as the big blue 20-inch housings, are cheap and easy to service but limited in contact time. Large tank systems hold a cubic foot or more of granular or catalytic carbon and run for years between media changes. Catalytic carbon is the choice for chloramine, which standard carbon removes poorly. Carbon handles taste, odor, chlorine and many organic chemicals, but not hardness, nitrate or dissolved minerals.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Reverse osmosis</strong> uses household pressure to force water across a very tight membrane and removes most dissolved solids, including lead, arsenic, fluoride, nitrate, many PFAS compounds and salts. Units certified to NSF/ANSI 58 have verified performance. RO is slow and sends a share of water to the drain, so it is used at the sink, not for the whole house. Tankless models with built-in pumps reduce wait times and waste ratios.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Softeners</strong> use ion exchange resin to swap calcium and magnesium for sodium or potassium. They prevent scale but do not filter anything else. <strong>Iron filters</strong> oxidize dissolved iron, manganese and hydrogen sulfide, often with an air pocket in the tank, then backwash the particles away. <strong>UV systems</strong> disinfect by scrambling microbial DNA, but only work on clear, sediment-free water, so they always go last in the chain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sizing for Flow and Capacity
          </h2>

          <p>
            An undersized whole-house system is the most common cause of complaints about low water pressure. Size it to peak demand: how many fixtures could run at once. A shower draws about 2 gallons per minute, a washing machine 2 to 3, a dishwasher 1 to 2 and a kitchen faucet about 1.5. A two-bathroom house with a laundry running can easily hit 8 to 10 gpm. Choose a system rated at or above that peak, not the average.
          </p>

          <p>
            Port size matters too. Most homes have three-quarter-inch or one-inch main lines. Putting a system with three-quarter-inch ports on a one-inch line creates a restriction. Large homes with three or more bathrooms usually need one-inch ports and a system rated for 12 gpm or more. Manufacturers usually sell the same system in several sizes keyed to bathroom count, so pick by bathroom count and check the flow rating against your calculation.
          </p>

          <p>
            Capacity is the other half. Whole-house carbon tanks are rated in gallons or years, commonly 500,000 to 1,000,000 gallons for a full tank, which at 100 gallons per person per day works out to several years for a family of four. Cartridge systems are rated much lower, commonly 50,000 to 100,000 gallons for the carbon stage. Sediment cartridges have no fixed rating; they last until pressure drop tells you they are full.
          </p>

          <p>
            For under-sink RO, look at rated gallons per day and the tank or pump design. Tank units rated for 50 to 75 gallons per day fill a storage tank slowly and dispense from it, so you get a few gallons quickly and then wait. Tankless units rated for 400 to 800 gallons per day produce on demand and fill a glass in seconds. Waste ratio, the amount sent to drain per gallon produced, ranges from about 3 to 1 on older tank units to near 1 to 1 or better on modern tankless ones.
          </p>

