        <div id="well-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. A Plan for Well Water
          </h2>

          <p>
            Well systems are built in a specific order, and getting the order wrong ruins equipment. The usual sequence is sediment filtration first, then iron and manganese removal, then a softener if hardness is high, then carbon for taste, and UV disinfection last. Each stage protects the next: iron fouls softener resin, hardness scales UV quartz sleeves, and any cloudiness lets microbes hide from UV light.
          </p>

          <p>
            Iron is the defining problem for many wells. Under about 1 ppm, a softener can handle small amounts. Between roughly 1 and 7 ppm, an air-injection oxidizing filter is the standard answer, and it also deals with moderate hydrogen sulfide. Above that, or with iron bacteria, chemical injection with chlorine or hydrogen peroxide and a contact tank is typically required. These systems backwash, so they need a drain and an electrical outlet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bacteria call for a specific response. If coliform shows up, the first step is shock chlorinating the well and finding the source, such as a damaged well cap or poor grouting. If contamination is ongoing, a UV system sized to your peak flow provides continuous disinfection. Look for NSF/ANSI 55 Class A for systems that claim disinfection of contaminated water. UV lamps need replacing about once a year whether or not they still glow.
          </p>

          <p>
            Arsenic, uranium and nitrate are drinking-water problems on wells, and an under-sink RO system certified for those contaminants is usually more practical than whole-house treatment. Retest after installing anything. A system you cannot prove works is just a tank in the basement. If you also battle damp in the space where the equipment lives, our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a> helps protect the electronics on control valves and UV ballasts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="certifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Certifications Worth Checking
          </h2>

          <p>
            Filtration marketing is full of percentages with no context. Certifications cut through it, because an accredited body such as NSF, IAPMO, WQA or CSA has confirmed the claim and audits the factory. Each standard covers a specific kind of device and a specific list of contaminants, so a certification is only useful if it lists the substance you care about. Look up the product in the certifier's online database, not just on the box.
          </p>

          <p>
            For carbon filters, <strong>NSF/ANSI 42</strong> covers chlorine, taste, odor and particulates, and <strong>NSF/ANSI 53</strong> covers health contaminants such as lead, cysts, VOCs and, in newer listings, PFOA and PFOS. <strong>NSF/ANSI 401</strong> adds incidental compounds like certain pharmaceuticals and herbicides. <strong>NSF/ANSI 58</strong> is the reverse osmosis standard, covering TDS reduction and optional claims for arsenic, nitrate, fluoride and others. <strong>NSF/ANSI 55</strong> applies to UV disinfection, and <strong>NSF/ANSI 372</strong> confirms low-lead materials.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whole-house systems are a weak spot. Many large tank systems are tested to NSF protocols by a third-party lab without holding a full listing, because the certification process for point-of-entry systems is expensive and slow. That is not automatically a red flag, but it means you should ask for the actual lab report and check which contaminants and challenge levels were used. A report that only covers chlorine at a modest level tells you little about anything else.
          </p>

          <p>
            For drinking-water systems at the sink, insist on a real listing. Under-sink RO and carbon block filters are widely certified, so there is little reason to accept a unit that is not. If your test shows lead or PFAS, the data sheet should show reduction of that contaminant at the standard's challenge level, along with the rated capacity in gallons. Replace the filter at that capacity, because the certification only covers performance up to it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Installation: DIY or Plumber?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Under-sink systems are a reasonable DIY job for anyone comfortable with basic plumbing. A carbon block filter usually connects to the cold-water supply with a tee adapter and needs a dedicated faucet, which may mean drilling the sink or countertop. RO units add a drain saddle on the sink drain line, and some need a nearby outlet for a booster pump. Budget a couple of hours and read the manual first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whole-house systems are a bigger commitment. You will cut into the main line, install a bypass loop with three valves so you can service the system without losing water, and add shutoffs on each side. Copper needs soldering or push-fit fittings, and PEX needs the right crimp or expansion tools. Many local codes require a permit for work on the main supply line. If any of that sounds unfamiliar, a plumber typically charges $300 to $1,000 to install a standard system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grounding is a detail people miss. Many homes use metal water pipes as part of the electrical grounding path. Inserting a plastic filter housing or a plastic bypass valve can interrupt that path. Code-compliant installs add a <strong>bonding jumper</strong> across the system with clamps on each side. It is a cheap wire and clamp, and skipping it can leave metal plumbing unbonded. If you are doing other electrical work, our <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">whole house surge protector guide</a> covers the main panel side of the same project.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan the location before buying. Whole-house tanks are tall and heavy when full, and backwashing models need a drain within reach and an outlet. Systems should sit indoors, out of freezing temperatures and direct sun, ideally after the pressure tank on wells and before the water heater. Leave clearance to change cartridges and lift the tank head. Cramming a system into a closet makes every future service visit miserable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Upfront and Lifetime Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Prices vary widely, and the sticker price is only part of the story. At the time of writing, a three-stage cartridge whole-house system runs roughly $250 to $600. A tank-style whole-house carbon system costs roughly $1,000 to $2,500. Well water combination systems with iron filtration and UV land roughly $1,500 to $4,000 before installation. Under-sink RO ranges from about $200 for a basic tank unit to $600 or more for tankless models, and countertop RO roughly $300 to $500.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ongoing costs flip the ranking. Cartridge whole-house systems need new sediment and carbon cartridges every three to twelve months, often $100 to $250 a year. Tank systems need only a sediment pre-filter for years, then a media change costing a few hundred dollars every five to ten years. Under-sink RO pre-filters run roughly $40 to $100 a year, with a membrane every two to four years. UV lamps cost roughly $60 to $150 a year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run a ten-year number before you choose. A $400 cartridge system with $200 a year of filters costs $2,400 over a decade. A $1,600 tank system with a $150 sediment budget and one media change might cost about the same, and it needs less attention. If you are likely to forget filter changes, the system with the fewest service intervals usually delivers better water in practice.
          </p>

