        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. A Realistic Maintenance Schedule
          </h2>

          <p>
            Install pressure gauges before and after a whole-house system. They cost a few dollars and give you the best signal for when a sediment cartridge needs changing: when the pressure drop across the system grows by about 10 to 15 psi beyond its clean baseline, the cartridge is loading up. Without gauges, you usually notice only when the shower turns weak.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set calendar reminders based on the manual. A typical schedule: sediment cartridges every three to six months, carbon cartridges every six to twelve months, RO pre-filters every six to twelve months, RO membrane every two to four years, UV lamp every twelve months and quartz sleeve cleaning at the same time. Tank media lasts years, but check the control valve settings once a year and after a power outage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sanitize when you change filters. Turn off the supply, relieve pressure, clean the housings with a mild unscented bleach solution or the manufacturer's sanitizer, rinse thoroughly and lubricate O-rings with food-grade silicone grease. A dry or twisted O-ring is the main cause of slow leaks after a cartridge change. Keep a spare O-ring on hand, because the original eventually flattens.
          </p>

          <p>
            Retest yearly. Filtration media does not announce failure, and a fresh lab test or a tap lead test confirms the system still does its job. On wells, test after any flooding or long power outage. Keep a log near the system with install dates, filter changes and test results, which helps a plumber diagnose problems quickly and helps a buyer if you sell the house.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying before testing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Systems are built for specific contaminants. Without a utility report or lab test, buyers pick by marketing and often end up with carbon when they needed a softener, or a softener when they needed lead removal. A lab panel costs less than one replacement filter set and decides the purchase for you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Undersizing a whole-house system</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A system rated for 7 gpm in a home that peaks at 12 gpm drops pressure every time two showers and the washer run. Count fixtures, add up flow at peak, and match port size to the main line. Sizing to bathroom count, then checking against the flow math, avoids the problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the bypass loop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Without a bypass, every cartridge change or valve repair shuts off water to the whole house. Three valves and a few fittings let you service the system while the house keeps running, and they let you isolate the system if it starts leaking. Plumbers install them by default for a reason.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the bonding jumper</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many homes rely on metal water pipes for electrical grounding. Plastic filter housings break that path unless a bonding jumper is clamped across the system. It is a cheap length of copper wire and two clamps, and it keeps metal plumbing properly bonded as code requires. Check before you close up the job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting UV before sediment and iron filtration</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                UV light only disinfects water it can see through. Sediment, iron and hardness shadow microbes and coat the quartz sleeve. Always place UV last in the treatment chain, after sediment, iron and softening stages, and clean the sleeve when you change the lamp each year.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which home water filtration setup suits most households?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For city water, a whole-house catalytic carbon tank such as the SpringWell CF1, paired with an under-sink reverse osmosis unit for drinking water, covers most concerns. If budget is tight, a three-stage cartridge system like the iSpring WGB32B handles sediment and chlorine. Base the final choice on your utility report or lab test rather than marketing claims.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a whole-house filter remove lead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most whole-house carbon systems are not certified for lead, and lead often leaches from solder, faucets and fittings downstream of where a whole-house filter sits. The reliable fix is an NSF/ANSI 53 or 58 certified filter at the kitchen tap, plus replacing lead service lines where possible. Some whole-house systems offer lead-specific stages, so check certifications before relying on them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a whole-house system lower my water pressure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A correctly sized system with clean filters causes only a small pressure drop. Problems come from undersized systems, ports smaller than the main line, or clogged sediment cartridges. Match the rated flow to your household peak demand, install pressure gauges before and after, and change sediment filters when the pressure difference grows noticeably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a softener and a filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They do different jobs. A softener removes the calcium and magnesium that cause scale, and a carbon filter removes chlorine, taste and many chemicals. If your water is hard and chlorinated, you need both. Install the carbon filter before the softener on chloramine or chlorine water, since chlorine degrades softener resin over time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does professional installation cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At the time of writing, plumbers typically charge roughly $150 to $400 to install an under-sink system and $300 to $1,000 for a standard whole-house filter. Well systems with backwashing tanks, iron filters and UV can run higher, especially if new drains or outlets are needed. Get quotes that include a bypass loop and bonding jumper.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is reverse osmosis water safe to drink long term?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. RO removes minerals as well as contaminants, but most dietary calcium and magnesium comes from food rather than water. Some people prefer the taste of water with minerals, and many RO systems offer a remineralization stage. Keep filters current, because an exhausted pre-filter can let chlorine reach and damage the membrane.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I filter well water with the same system as city water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. City water mainly needs chlorine or chloramine removal. Well water often needs sediment, iron, manganese and hardness treatment, and sometimes UV disinfection. Get a full lab panel first, then build the system in the right order: sediment, iron removal, softener, carbon, and UV last. A city-water carbon tank alone usually fails quickly on a well.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
